    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d33">

                <pb facs="0058" n="44" sameAs="frus:frus1949v04/pg44"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.20/1–2449: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Holmes</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-01-24T18:00:00+00:00">January 24, 1949—6
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>286. In compliance Department’s 233,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d33-f1"/> saw Bevin
                    today and communicated substance reftel and
                        Deptel 224 January 19.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d33-f2"/> He said that he would have to consult cabinet but gave me
                    the impression that he reluctantly agreed to Italy’s initial inclusion Atlantic
                    Pact. Jebb<anchor n="3" xml:id="d33-f3"/> was present and in conversation with
                    him following interview he stated that if we and French insist Italy, British
                    will not hold out. Both Bevin and Jebb seemed concerned that inclusion Italy
                    would drain available equipment from continental powers. Jebb inquired whether
                    Italy could be warned not to expect important allocations at least for some
                    time. I replied that I could not answer this question but that I felt sure our
                    position would be that equipment should be allocated where strategically most
                    needed.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 286, repeated Paris 56, The Hague 11, Brussels 14, Rome 12.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Holmes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d33-f1" n="1" xml:id="d33-f1-content">
                <p>Printed as telegram No. 61 to Brussels, January 21, <ref target="#pg41">p.
                        41</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d33-f2" n="2" xml:id="d33-f2-content">
                <p>Printed as telegram No. 57 to Brussels, January 19, <ref target="#pg40">p.
                        40</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d33-f3" n="3" xml:id="d33-f3-content">
                <p>Sir H. M. Gladwyn Jebb, British Assistant Under-Secretary of State and U.K.
                    Representative on Brussels Treaty Permanent Commission with personal rank of
                    Ambassador.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
